Chapter: NegationAbutment
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The minus binds to whatever it abuts. All four spacings appear here, and
 each line answers a different number, so no line can pass by accident.

 arg-lit and arg-only are the shapes that used to be subtraction and would
 not compile at all. three-args is the one that pins how TIGHTLY a negation
 binds: the negated argument is a single atom, so `three 1 -2 3` is three
 arguments and not `three 1 (-(2 3))`. left-abut and hyphen-digit pin the
 other two spacings, and arrow pins the one exception, since a hyphen
 before a greater-than is the arrow and not a name character.

Section: Helpers

  one : Integer -> Integer
  one (n) = n

  two : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  two (a) (b) = a + b

  three : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  three (a) (b) (c) = a * 100 + b * 10 + c

  neg-var : Integer -> Integer
  neg-var (x) = two 1 -x

  a- : Integer -> Integer
  a- (n) = n + 1

  x-2 : Integer
  x-2 = 7

  arrowed : Integer->Integer
  arrowed (n) = n * 2

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni ("arg-lit: " & show (two 1 -2))
   print-line-uni ("arg-only: " & show (one -5))
   print-line-uni ("arg-var: " & show (neg-var 3))
   print-line-uni ("three-args: " & show (three 1 -2 3))
   print-line-uni ("sub: " & show (7 - 2))
   print-line-uni ("left-abut: " & show (a- 2))
   print-line-uni ("hyphen-digit: " & show x-2)
   print-line-uni ("paren: " & show (two 1 (-2)))
   print-line-uni ("arrow: " & show (arrowed 4))
  end
